ely train our  models and tune our hyper parameters was  increasing as the team which was  maturing and our understanding of our  domain was as well uh and we get to this  point where we have a model with a 99 f1  score uh with 99.5 percent recall or  greater than that and less than 99  precision and so what this means if  you're not familiar with these metrics  is that with a 99.5 recall you would  expect only one in every 200 scans of a  gun to be missed by this model to not be  detected here and then uh we presented  these statistics to a customer  deployed this model out into the field  and the model missed a very obvious case  uh something that looks almost exactly  like what you see on the right where a  gun was just placed in the center of a  bin this is like a x-ray scanner that  you'll find at an airport or something  like that and so imagine those plastic  bins gun was just placed in the center  of the bin run through the machines it  goes through our system and sure enough  miss detection this is a single  occurrence that has pretty extreme  consequences for a mission critical ml  product uh you lose tr you lose trust  with your customer and lost trust is  irreparable uh to some extent there is  very very difficult time reestablishing  trust that you've lost by poor  performance and particularly by poor  performance that blindsided you  on our end this kicked off a long time  spent investigating why this happened  collecting additional data to make sure  that  we're training models for which this  wouldn't happen and then retraining  those models and validating that this  doesn't happen again and then  in our processes we collected all this  additional data and we made sure that it  was being used in training and those  samples were included in testing but  that whole process provides us with no  guarantee that this bug won't reappear  and as we're training models daily and  pushing new models out weekly uh  things like this silent regressions and  behavior are something that you really  need to be careful about  and  what this really comes down to is that  the current ml evaluation approach that  we have is falling short uh you'll spend  a lot of time collecting and annotating  data and you will  arbitrarily split that into some  training set validation set and then  test set that you finally use to produce  these metrics and so you train models  and they get better and better here  they're they trend closer and closer  towards zero loss and especially if you  have things that are getting up around  the 99 percent error area you are  succeeding in a lot of cases and failing  in relatively few cases here and you  keep iterating on your models you try  different architectures you try  different hyper parameters and you watch  these loss plots get better and better  and then you produce a global metric  like 99 f1 score that is produced by  performing inference on all of this uh  benchmark data set your test split there  you perform inference on that and  produce some global metric that says you  know we're 99 good at detection in this  case uh and that metric fails to tell  you really anything meaningful about the  model's behavior here and uh typically  since when you're iterating from one  model to the next you want to make sure  that you have uh you're comparing apples  to apples here you'll typically freeze  this benchmark data set you'll you know  set it at the beginning of the year and  then you'll do the next six months of  model development with the same frozen  benchmark and your main objective is  optimizing that global metric on that  frozen benchmark here and that really  fails to tell you anything about what  you can expect from your model in the  field  and one of the main reasons for that  is something that's been labeled by  research as the hidden stratification  problem in the past couple of years  and to understand what the hidden  stratification problem is uh let's look  at the specific case that motivated this  paper here where a researcher was trying  to reproduce some results from another  paper  uh trying to classify the presence or  absence of cancer in chest x-rays and he  was able to use the same model as the  other paper and reproduce the same  results here and uh you know get the  exact same numbers and it seemed like  that was uh you know a successful  reproduction of that paper but  when he looked into it as a researcher  he found that there were statistically  meaningful subclasses within the  main class of chest x-ray with or  without scan and so when you're training  this classifier you'd feed in this chest  x-ray and you are training against the  ground truth uh has cancer or doesn't  have cancer and you're training a  classifier model to try to discriminate  between images that do and do not have  cancer but that single label has cancer  has a lot of subclasses within it hidden  strata that are meaningful when it comes  to the predictive ability of the model  to accurately classify them and in this  particular case this researcher found  that  a majority of the successfully predicted  examples here these chest x-rays were  examples that had something called a  chest strain which is a  palliative procedure that's used to  reduce some of the symptoms of late  stage chest cancer and the model was  actually learning to use the presence of  a chest strain as a predictive indicator  and when he removed these scans with  chest strains you'll see here on this  receiver operating characteristic plot  over here that's plotting true positive  rate versus false positive rate as you  vary threshold is a very confidence  threshold uh and so something that looks  more like an l like an upside down l is  a better performer has a better ability  to uh accurately classify examples  the statistically the the meaningless  case the ones with chest strains where  the model was just looking at the  presence of a chest drain was very very  good it had generally quite good  performance the area under the roc curve  being 0.94 meaning it's getting close to  a perfect classifier there  the aggregate metric here was a little  bit less but still quite good you know  0.87 auc and the actually meaningful  case the one you care about in a  clinical setting the classification of  hazard has not cancer for images that do  not have chest strains was severely  underperforming here you have your auc  of 0.77 which means that this has very  little utility as a classifier and is  very far away from something that you  would expect to use in a clinical  setting here and this is a particularly  poignant example of the hidden  stratification problem but this exists  all over uh pretty much any domain that  you'll be trying to develop a model for  uh global metrics fail to capture the  full picture here this auc that you have  on all of these uh on all of the  examples aggregated doesn't actually  tell you what your performance in the  cases you care about is and typically  models will exhibit highly variable  performance across these different  subclasses  and since you're just looking at some  global metric here you can uh  improve greatly in one subclass that you  don't care about like the one with chest  drains and regress in one that uh you do  care about like no chest strains and  have that masked have that failure be  masked this regression be silent because  you're looking at only the average of  those subclasses  and this is a problem that exists across  the industry and  uh for the same reasons a lot of  important questions when it comes to  deploying models and trying to  successfully build ml products are  extremely difficult to answer if you've  been involved in this effort as an  engineer a scientist a pm an engineering  lead  a business person even a customer trying  to buy them  you have likely been on one end or maybe  both ends of these questions and seen  how difficult they can be to answer  basic things like what are the models  failure modes when can i expect it to  succeed if i'm pushing a new model what  has improved or what has regressed  if my model has improved by some you  know arbitrary delta in global metric  how how is this improvement distributed  is it can i expect better performance in  all scenarios or is it localized to a  particular scenario  if i have a certain deployment with  certain conditions and i have multiple  models maybe a couple of new models that  i've been training and some past models  that i've released which model is going  to be best for those circumstances which  model should we deploy and then the big  question which is very very difficult to  answer typically how do i know if my  model is ready to be deployed and this  is the scary one that you really like to  have a good answer for but often you  don't  the way that we solve this problem is  with a paradigm shift to something that  we call test case-based evaluation here  this is it involves a fundamentally  different way of thinking about your  problem uh it takes from a world where  we have a test set represented like what  we have on the left which might be  unbalanced across subclasses might be  arbitrarily split you know with our  train valve test split we'll take some  random sampling of this big bucket that  we're using to both train and evaluate  our models uh and we'll freeze this  benchmark data set and have you know  these different colors might represent  different subclasses we'll just have  those randomly scattered around in this  test set  and test case based evaluation involves  migrating to a world where you actually  sort those colors into different test  cases and you take your one benchmark  and break that benchmark data set into  many different more tightly scoped test  cases that are attempting to assess  behavior in a very specific scenario  here and when we're looking at the case  of the x-ray gun detector some of the  fine-grained test cases that we would  care about are things like gun in an  empty bin uh a top view maybe a front  view looking at the sensor or a back  view looking away from the sensor  guns in low occlusion environments guns  in high occlusion environments high blur  if you have an old or an uncalibrated  x-ray sensor  and these would be the sort of test  cases that you would be looking to  create in order to understand your  model's behavior  and when you create these test cases and  evaluate your models on them you get  high resolution test results here and to  illustrate that we'll look at this  example where we have two models labeled  model a and model b on the left and the  right we've produced a global metric on  our benchmark data set where model a is  97.8 let's say this is f1 score the  harmonic mean of precision and recall  but it could be anything you know  whatever metric you you use as your  north star as a team and model b has  99.2 on this global metric when we look  at this at the test case level here we  see that uh model a has quite good  performance across gun and empty bin  back view front view low occlusion and  very very poor performance in high  occlusion and high blur uh  model b on the other hand has middling  performance in gun and empty bin and  then good performance across back view  front view low occlusion and also pretty  good performance in high occlusion and  high blur but  all of these different scenarios don't  matter to your product equally  you should assign some product criteria  to them where we can say things like a  gun and empty bin is a critical behavior  that our product needs to have and when  you use that you can say based off of my  product criteria of this being a  critical example these others being  important and then these bottom two high  occlusion high blur being irrelevant  you can make the call that model a which  has the lower global metric is actually  better aligned with the objectives that  you're trying to achieve here and then  you can choose to deploy that model and  likely achieve better results in the  field  so the process here is something that we  call test centric ml model development  here where in in the middle of all of  the moving parties that are involved in  training and deploying ml models  the central item is your tests this  means that for your ml engineering team  uh the tests are what's used to guide  development and make sure that any  improvements in the models are  accurately reflected for the product  management team the tests are what  you're using to actually encode your  product objectives if you care about  behavior in a certain scenario you  reflect that with a test case of that  certain scenario and then you're able to  accurately assess whether or not you are  aligning with your objectives or not  and for bd and sales and external  parties tests are how you communicate  the behaviors and the capabilities of  your models here  and that's extremely important because  everything that you know about your  model behavior  before you push things to production you  learn from your tests uh this means that  if there is anything that you can expect  to see if there's any behavior or any  particular shortcoming that you might  have in your model the only way that  you'll surface that before you push  things into production and maybe get  burned is by good tests that will  uncover that  and what this means also is that your  testing data is more important than your  training data and this might seem  backwards to a lot of people that have  been thinking about ml or training ml  models because you obviously need more  training data or that's uh what the  current thinking is you need more  training data and then your testing data  should just be some small set that  validates learned behaviors from that  training example  uh that's backwards the training data is  ultimately an implementation detail the  training data if you have a model that  can acquire a certain set of behaviors  with 100 000 examples 