  where we have roughly even  representation uh across each of these  different subclasses a through f and so  our data set has probably been very  carefully put together it's been uh  manually curated to make sure that we're  representing each of these different  subclasses well  uh  model the model on the left  has relatively consistent performance  across each of the different subclasses  and the model on the right has extreme  spikes and extreme troughs in its  behavior it excels at subclasses e and f  and is quite deficient at subclasses a b  and c and both of these models can  produce the same global metric despite  having wildly different behaviors and  this is why looking at something that  says my f1 score on class x will never  tell you what you really need to know to  know you know can i deploy this model in  a deployment environment that's going to  see lots of subclasses a b and c you  just can't answer this question even if  you have a very very carefully put  together  test data set that has an even  distribution  it's important to say here sometimes it  feels  a little bit too  clear to say but it's important to  always reiterate that your metrics are  entirely dependent on the test data that  produces them these performance metrics  that you're producing are a function of  both the model and of the test data and  that means that uh you know when the  model is changing and when you're  reporting a metric with a new model the  only way that you can actually  understand what those metrics mean is if  you understand the data that backs it  and very very few people have a full  understanding of the data set that's  used to train these models i would say  that it's probably built in the head of  the ml engineer who's been leading that  particular training push uh if he or she  actually even has a full understanding  of what's in that test data set and how  it's distributed other people like  engineering managers or product managers  or business people or 